NAME
    wdesk - the desktop on the resident window-manager kernel

SYNOPSIS
    wdesk [-r]

DESCRIPTION
    A desktop whose window manager lives in the OPERATING SYSTEM, not in
    the program. The kernel (os/wmkernel_body.asm, folded into the OS
    image) holds the window records and runs the event loop; it is
    reached through syscalls right after SYS_EXEC -- SYS_WKINIT ($2027),
    SYS_WKOPEN ($202A), SYS_WKREPAINT ($202D), SYS_WKRUN ($2030),
    SYS_WKSAVE ($2033), SYS_WKLOAD ($2036), SYS_WKPATH ($2039) -- and is
    resident from boot, so wdesk itself is only a thin LAUNCHER: it
    records the SHAPES scene into a card-resident command list, opens
    its windows, points the launch key at paint, and hands over to
    SYS_WKRUN.

    THE POINT: the TPA holds one program, so launching an application
    replaces the desktop in memory (System-1 style, see man desk). With
    `desk`, that destroys the window manager -- the app must re-exec
    desk from scratch. With wdesk the manager is in the OS: press 'l',
    paint loads OVER wdesk, and when you quit paint it calls SYS_WKRUN
    (it was launched with -w) and the desktop reappears with every
    window exactly where it was. The records survived in the OS, the
    SHAPES picture survived on the card (CLRUN replays it), and nothing
    was reloaded.

    THE MENU BAR (the white strip across the top, drawn by wdesk itself,
    not the kernel):

        DESK      PAINT      CLOSE      QUIT

    The desktop and its windows are the KERNEL's; the menu bar is the
    CLIENT's. wdesk drives the kernel one event at a time (SYS_WKEVENT):
    the kernel handles the window mechanics and hands wdesk the keys it
    does not own AND any mouse click in the bar's top rows (with the
    cursor column, via SYS_WKARG) -- wdesk maps the column to a word.

    CLICK a bar word, or press its letter -- both do the same:

    PAINT  or  L      launch paint OVER wdesk. paint runs in the same
                      TPA; the kernel keeps the windows alive. Quitting
                      paint (it was launched with -w) re-runs "wdesk -r",
                      which redraws the SAME windows and this menu bar --
                      the desktop survives the launch.
    CLOSE  or  C      close the top (focused) window (SYS_WKCLOSE)
    QUIT   or  Q      leave the desktop for the shell (Ctrl-D also quits)

    The FOCUSED window is the top one: WHITE title bar, the others GREY.

    TAB               cycle focus: raise the bottom window to the top
    arrow keys        move the focused (top) window 8 px
    mouse             press ANY window to raise and focus it; hold and
                      drag to move it, release to place it. Press the
                      small black CLOSE BOX at the left of a title bar
                      to close that window. Xterm SGR reports; see
                      man paint, MOUSE.

OPTIONS
    -r  RESUME: the windows are already in the resident kernel (a
        launched app is returning), so skip init and just redraw. Not
        for interactive use -- it is how paint -w brings the desktop
        back.

NOTES
    This is the resident-kernel rung of the window-system ladder
    (docs/p8x-wm-design.md). The full-featured standalone `desk` -- menus,
    close boxes, the FILES browser, the live TERM, the VIEW picture
    window -- is unchanged and still the richer desktop today; those
    features migrate into the kernel in later rungs. Boot is unchanged:
    monitor, B, the shell -- the GUI is opt-in by command.

    A program launched by the kernel gets the arguments in the SYS_WKPATH
    string. paint understands -w (resume the resident desktop on quit)
    as well as -d (re-exec desk). A program that ignores both simply
    exits to the shell; type wdesk to return.

SEE ALSO
